{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Bidirectional Markdown ⇄ Jira rich-text sync (Cloud ADF + DC wiki)\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe Jira reconciler already routes ticket descriptions/comments through the\n`RichTextCodec` seam (epic `e369`, story J3 / `aac8`), but the **current encode\nis plain-text-structured, not Markdown-aware**: Cloud emits a plain-text ADF\n(`adf.text_to_adf`, one paragraph per block) via `acli.py:205` / `acli_cli_ops.py:369`,\nand DC's `WikiTextCodec` is an **identity** codec (plain string through). So the\nMarkdown structure authors write (headings, checklists, code, lists) shows up as\nliteral source in Jira, and rich text authored in Jira reads as noise in rebar.\nWe want each client to receive a native rich representation — **ADF** for Jira\nCloud, **wiki markup** for Jira Data Center — without a sync echo loop that would\nratchet Jira-specific formatting back into the store or erode content over\nrepeated passes. Validated over the real historical corpus (1,801 tickets /\n5,730 bodies, authoritative `rebar export`) by six de-risking experiments plus a\nmarklas-vs-`adf.py` parity experiment, recorded in session log\n`5eb0-52a3-45f0-43e5`.\n\n## Approach\nTwo asymmetric codecs behind one state-based echo-safety layer, built ON the\nexisting `RichTextCodec` seam (`adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py`), because the\ntwo Jira targets are fundamentally different formats:\n- **Cloud = truly bidirectional, lossless.** Make the existing `AdfCodec` /\n  `adf.py` encode **Markdown-aware** (it already emits ADF, just plain-text\n  paragraphs today). `marklas` round-trips Markdown↔ADF at **99.65% idempotent\n  round-trip** over the live corpus (5,730 bodies) — the structured-typed-node\n  pattern, inherently echo-safe under normalize-both-sides.\n- **DC = one-way MD→wiki render, bidirectional SYNC.** Replace `WikiTextCodec`'s\n  identity outbound with a segmenting renderer. Jira wiki is a lossy text markup\n  and no library round-trips rebar's punctuation-dense prose reliably (pandoc's\n  jira reader misparses `->`, ASCII tables, and regex, and provably erodes them\n  one char per pass). So DC renders one way over the units that are structurally\n  safe, passes dangerous units through verbatim, and prevents loops with\n  **state-based echo suppression** using the reconciler's existing per-binding\n  baseline (ADR 0026, `binding_store.py:102`) — NOT codec fixed-point. Genuine\n  human Jira edits are still detected against baseline and pulled back best-effort.\n\nEcho-safety is state-based for both clients; concurrency is last-write-wins over a\nthree-way state (baseline/local/remote) so neither side's changes are ever\nsilently destroyed. State additions obey epic `0303`'s churn discipline\n(change-gated, fixed-size, never a per-pass timestamp).\n\n## Dependencies / sequencing\nThis epic must not be claimed until its three in-flight prerequisites close:\n`0303` (tickets-store growth / committed-state format), `e369` (Jira-family DC\nseam), `3e73` (Cloud↔DC test parity). These are recorded as `depends_on` links.\n\n## Sequencing note — capability then cutover (observable change is intentional)\nThe two codec leaves deliver **capability only** and deliberately leave the live\nsend paths on today's plain/identity encode, so nothing observable changes on\ntheir merge. The **live cutover for BOTH clients** (rerouting the direct Cloud\n`adf.py` encode sites and the DC shared-mapper/CREATE composition through the\ncapable codecs), its **once-only re-emit migration of already-synced bodies**, the\n**second-pass convergence proof**, and a **flag-gated rollback** all land in the\nstate-echo-suppression story **together with the echo-safety mechanism** — never a\nhazard-before-safety window. This feature therefore **intentionally changes Cloud's\nand DC's observable rendering** (native rich text instead of literal Markdown\nsource) at that cutover, and **adds vendored third-party scope** (`marklas` under\n`src/rebar/_vendor/`, `pypandoc-binary`); both are in-scope and gated.\n\n## Child responsibilities (decomposition)\n- **Cloud Markdown↔ADF functions (pure only)** — add\n  `markdown_to_adf`/`adf_to_markdown`/`fit_markdown_to_adf_limit` to `adf.py`\n  (vendor/pin `marklas` with upstream sha + MIT LICENSE), including the combined\n  strong+strikethrough mark-order canonicalizer; tested over a vendored\n  `tests/fixtures/cloud_adf_corpus/`. Leaves `AdfCodec` a plain pass-through and\n  the live send sites unchanged (wiring is the cutover story). Ships\n  unit/corpus tests only — no codec change, no live cutover. Delivers the encoder\n  half of AC1.\n- **DC wiki renderer (pure module only)** — ship\n  `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py`: split body into blocks/list-items, render\n  structurally-safe units to wiki via pinned `pypandoc-binary`, substitute prose\n  arrows on the Markdown BEFORE pandoc, verbatim-wrap ASCII tables in `{noformat}`,\n  pass dangerous units through unchanged; identity-passthrough fallback when pandoc\n  is absent; tested over a vendored `tests/fixtures/dc_wiki_corpus/`. Leaves\n  `WikiTextCodec` fully identity and the live send unchanged (wiring is the cutover\n  story). Delivers the renderer half of AC2.\n- **DC robustness/perf hardening** — bound pandoc's per-field subprocess cost, add\n  a hard timeout with plain-text fallback on pathological input, and satisfy\n  pandoc GPL/SBOM compliance for the bundled binary. Delivers AC2 under production\n  constraints.\n- **Live cutover + state echo suppression + LWW conflict safety** — repoint the\n  Cloud `AdfCodec` ops to the Markdown-aware `adf.py` functions and wire the DC\n  `wiki_render` module into `WikiTextCodec`, then reroute the live send for both\n  clients (Cloud: the direct `adf.py` encode/fit/compare sites; DC: `to_wire`→\n  renderer and `normalize_outbound`→`decode_inbound(to_wire(t))`), updating the\n  Cloud + DC pin tests; prove second-pass convergence, and add a flag-gated\n  rollback; reuse the ADR-0026 baseline (observed-after-push canonical form) and\n  the codec's `normalize_outbound` compare op plus a change-gated inline\n  `rich_sha`; three-way LWW that always flags concurrent conflict; origin-gated\n  outbound so Jira-native constructs survive. Delivers the observable rendering\n  change plus AC3, AC4, AC5.\n- **Comment sync (append-only)** — persistent Jira-comment-ID map (replacing\n  body-equality identity); rebar→Jira creation and inbound new-comment mirroring\n  only (no edit/delete sync), rendered through the same codec seam; descriptions\n  cut over before comments. Delivers the comment portion of AC1/AC2.\n- **Live DC harness + cross-repo contract** — extend the existing Docker Jira DC\n  harness to assert rendered-HTML fidelity (not just wiki source), and add a\n  contract test pinning `normalize_rich_text` back-compat for the sibling\n  `agentic-dev-platform` importer. Delivers AC6.\n- **Comment sync (append-only)** — persistent Jira-comment-ID map (replacing\n  body-equality identity); rebar→Jira creation and inbound new-comment mirroring\n  only (no edit/delete sync), rendered through the same codec seam; descriptions\n  cut over before comments. Delivers the comment portion of AC1/AC2.\n- **Live DC harness + cross-repo contract** — extend the existing Docker Jira DC\n  harness to assert rendered-HTML fidelity (not just wiki source), and add a\n  contract test pinning `normalize_rich_text` back-compat for the sibling\n  `agentic-dev-platform` importer. Delivers AC6.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Cloud descriptions and comments sync as Markdown-aware ADF and round-trip\n  Markdown↔ADF with no perpetual re-sync over the historical corpus; verified by a\n  corpus idempotence test that asserts convergence (incl. the two known\n  combined-mark bodies).\n- [ ] DC descriptions and comments render to wiki for structurally-safe content\n  while remaining echo-safe with zero content erosion over ≥5 repeated passes,\n  including the known ASCII-table ratchet body; verified by a corpus echo-safety\n  test asserting no monotonic length decay.\n- [ ] A reconcile pass in which no body changed writes 0 new committed-state\n  entries and no per-pass timestamp attributable to this feature; verified by the\n  git-delta cost harness reproducing the ≤0.07 KB/version stable profile.\n- [ ] A concurrent edit on both sides is always detected and flagged rather than\n  silently overwritten; verified by the concurrent-conflict regression test over\n  the corpus (the proven 799/799-detected invariant).\n- [ ] A Jira-authored native construct (panel/mention/date) survives a subsequent\n  rebar-originated reconcile pass; verified by an origin-gating test.\n- [ ] The end-to-end behavior runs against a live Jira DC instance asserting\n  rendered HTML (via `?expand=renderedFields`), not just wiki source; verified by\n  the extended Docker DC harness CI job."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785811291831599001,"uuid":"e25e3c27-8d52-4562-92bd-16efede9fd1b"}